# width: 120
# The pin. A revealed column is paint, not layout — the marker at column
# 95 of the open file proves it, since an editor squeezed beside a
# docked 30-cell tree clips it. `view.sidebar_pin` is the one moment a
# relayout IS wanted: the column docks, the editor loses its 30 cells
# and the marker goes with them. Unpinning gives them back.
#
# (The click that OPENS a file and takes the panel with it is pinned by
# the unit test in src/app/sidebar_auto.zig, which can find the tree
# row by the hit its paint registered instead of guessing a cell.)
write .mnml/config.zon .{ .ui = .{ .sidebar = .auto, .sidebar_reveal_ms = 40, .sidebar_hide_ms = 4000 } }
write wide.txt "...............................................................................................ENDMARK\n"
write zz_marker.txt "nothing opens this\n"
open wide.txt
expect screen contains "ENDMARK"
hover 0 10
wait 200
expect screen contains "󰐃"
expect screen contains "zz_marker"
expect screen contains "ENDMARK"
# Pinned: the column is docked now, so the editor really is narrower.
command view.sidebar_pin
wait 200
expect screen contains "sidebar pinned"
expect screen contains "zz_marker"
expect screen lacks "ENDMARK"
# The pinned column has no strip and no pin chip — it is an ordinary
# docked column again.
expect screen lacks "󰐃"
# Unpinned: back to auto, the column goes, the editor has its width.
# (The pointer leaves column 0 first — that cell is the reveal zone
# again the moment the pin comes off.)
hover 90 20
command view.sidebar_pin
wait 200
expect screen contains "sidebar: auto-hide on"
expect screen lacks "zz_marker"
expect screen contains "ENDMARK"
